<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Torres Strait Islanders</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Torres Strait Islanders are the Indigenous Melanesian peoples of the islands of the Torres Strait between Cape <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> in Queensland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>, with cultures distinct from those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-australians">Aboriginal Australians</a>. Annexed to Queensland in the 1870s, the islands are part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. The Mabo case of 1992, brought by Meriam people, established native title in Australian law.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/torres-strait-islanders.jpg" alt="Torres Strait Islanders" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kelisi at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TorresStraitIslandsMap.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of Australia grew out of six British colonies founded between 1788, when the First Fleet established a penal settlement at Sydney Cove, and 1859, when Queensland was separated from New South Wales, on a continent inhabited by Aboriginal and <b>Torres Strait Islander</b> peoples for at least 50,000 years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Aboriginal Australians are the Indigenous peoples of the Australian mainland and many of its islands, apart from the distinct <b>Torres Strait Islanders</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/aboriginal-australians">Aboriginal Australians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The first recording was made on a wax cylinder in 1898 by Charles Samuel Myers and Alfred Cort Haddon during an expedition to record the music of the <b>Torres Strait Islanders</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/auld-lang-syne">Auld Lang Syne</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Taba Naba&quot; is a traditional children's song of the Torres Strait Islands, just north of the Australian mainland, sung in Meriam Mir, a language of the <b>Torres Strait Islanders</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/taba-naba">Taba Naba</a></li></ul></section>
